## Connect inputs, actions and events

| Role | Application responsibility | During model execution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `input` | Implement the read interface below, or supply a snapshot for this cycle | Sampled anew each cycle; read-only to the model, no implicit hold |
| `param` | Supply during construction/initialization; omissions use DSL defaults | Fixed per instance; generated getters need no callback implementation |
| `control` (`def`, often called var) | Optionally preset at initialization, then observe | Model-owned writable state; holds when not assigned |
| `output` | Read after a successful cycle and pass to an actuator if needed | Shares persistent storage with control; holds when not assigned |

`Vars` holds control/output state; only the DSL writes it. `Parameters` is copied and validated during initialization. In `InitOptions`, pair each supplied value with its presence flag; omitted values use DSL defaults. For inputs, implement the callbacks below or supply a complete typed `Inputs` snapshot. Every input is sampled once before execution, including unused inputs, and the same values are used for validation and actions.

Integer input callbacks write the generated signed 64-bit `Int`; floating input callbacks write finite `double` values. For declared inputs, missing providers, non-finite values and callbacks returning 0 fail the cycle. Input getters require an active cycle; parameter getters require an initialized instance.

{% if model.defines %}
| Role | DSL name | Access |
| --- | --- | --- |
{% for item in model.dynamic_inputs.values() %}| input | `{{ item.name }}` | `InputProvider.read_{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }}`; `get_input_{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }}` |
{% endfor %}{% for item in model.static_inputs.values() %}| param | `{{ item.name }}` | `InitOptions.parameters.{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }}` + `parameters_present.{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }}`; `get_param_{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }}` |
{% endfor %}{% for item in model.persistent_variables.values() %}| {{ item.role.value }} | `{{ item.name }}` | `Vars.{{ item.name | to_c_identifier }}` |
{% endfor %}

{% else %}
This model has no input, parameter, control or output declarations.
{% endif %}

### Callback responsibilities

| Interface | Invocation | Result and ownership |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `InputProvider.read_*` | Every declared input once per active cycle; explicit snapshots bypass the provider | Write the typed output and return 1; 0 aborts sampling. The table is copied; `user_data` is borrowed |
| `Hooks` | When an abstract lifecycle action executes; never during speculative validation | Returns void; uninstalled slots do nothing. The table and `user_data` are borrowed |
| `EventChecks` | Queried lazily when a transition needs an event; first result cached within the cycle | Nonzero means active; zero means inactive. Complete table required for eventful models; table and data are borrowed |

The callbacks in Quick Start share `AppData` through `user_data`. Keep hook/event tables in the owning object or another scope that outlives all cycles; registering a pointer to a local table that goes out of scope is invalid. Inside an action, read `ctx->vars` for that action’s observation and use `data->owner->get_input_*()` / `get_param_*()` through the wrapper saved in `AppData`. Do not write persistent state from callbacks; express changes in the DSL. Send actuator outputs after a successful cycle when they must reflect committed state.

C++ input/action/event callbacks use the C signatures exposed by `Wrapper::InputProvider`, `Wrapper::Hooks` and the context aliases. There are no virtual callback methods to override. Use `user_data` to reach your application object; invoke machine operations through the wrapper.

