## Initialization and recovery

Configure `InitOptions` before the constructor/init call in Quick Start. A nonzero `parameters_present` or `vars_present` field selects the corresponding value and bypasses its DSL initializer. Omitted parameters and persistent values use DSL defaults. Cold reinitialization starts a new run and clears installed callbacks; reinstall them before the next cycle. Parameters cannot be changed during a run.

To preset control/output, insert this fragment before the initialization call in Quick Start. Each presence flag selects that value; model execution may then overwrite it. Leave a flag zero to keep the DSL initializer.

<!-- example:initial-values -->

```cpp
{% if model.persistent_variables %}{% for item in model.persistent_variables.values() %}options.vars.{{ item.name | to_c_identifier }} = 1;
options.vars_present.{{ item.name | to_c_identifier }} = 1;
{% endfor %}{% else %}/* This model has no persistent variables to preset. */
{% endif %}
```

### Hot Start

This fragment continues after a successful cycle in Quick Start. It captures state, all persistent values and parameters from the same checkpoint and restores them without replaying entry actions. For saved checkpoints, supply those saved values instead. Do not replace checkpoint parameters with current defaults or new configuration. A composite target starts in initial-transition wait mode. Hot start preserves installed providers, hooks and event checks; a failed hot start preserves the previous run. Serialize snapshot capture and restoration with all other access to the instance.

<!-- example:hot-start -->

```cpp
Wrapper::StateId state = machine.current_state_id();
Wrapper::Vars initial_vars = *machine.vars();
Wrapper::Parameters parameters;
memset(&parameters, 0, sizeof(parameters));
{% for item in model.static_inputs.values() %}parameters.{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }} = machine.get_param_{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }}();
{% endfor %}
if (state == {{ a }}_INVALID_STATE_ID) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Cannot restore a completed machine as a live checkpoint\n");
    return 3;
}
if (!machine.hot_start(state, initial_vars, parameters)) {
    fprintf(stderr, "hot start failed: %s\n", machine.last_error());
    return 3;
}
```

## Results, failures and lifetime

Initialization and cycle return 1 on success and 0 on failure; inspect `last_error()` immediately after failure. A C++ constructor reports failure through `last_error()` instead of throwing. Sampling/validation/execution errors do not commit persistent values or `last_inputs`. Fix the cause and retry a cycle; it acquires fresh input. External reads, logs and action side effects are not rolled back. A successful Delta cycle holds persistent state and publishes its input snapshot.

Read control/output through `vars()` after success. This returns instance-owned storage, not a historical copy; copy `Vars` to retain values. Input getters are valid only during a cycle; read `last_inputs()` afterwards and copy `Inputs` when retaining history. Context pointers are valid only inside their callback. Getter calls require an initialized live instance. Calls after termination do not sample inputs. The wrapper owns its native instance by value; do not pass its `native_handle()` to a C destroy function.

