| `native_handle()` | Borrow the underlying C instance for explicit interoperability |
| `set_event_checks(checks, user_data)` | Install a complete event table; check last_error |

For each input/parameter, use the exact getter names listed in Integration. The parameter-free hot-start overload is valid only for models without parameters. Internal frame, stack and scratch fields are runtime-owned even where visible in the header.

### `Wrapper::ExecutionContext`

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `state_id` | Public call-context state id; compare with generated `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_STATE_*` macros |
| `vars` | Read-only snapshot pointer to persistent machine variables |
| `action_id` | Resolved abstract target action id; compare with generated `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_ACTION_*` macros |
| `action_stage_id` | Lifecycle stage id reported for this callsite; compare with `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_STAGE_*` |
| `active_leaf_state_id` | Active leaf-state id visible to the callsite |
| `call_stage_id` | Lifecycle stage id of the concrete callsite before resolving `ref` chains |
| `abstract_target_id` | Resolved abstract action id after following `ref` chains |
| `named_ref_id` | Named `ref` action id when the callsite itself is a named `ref`, otherwise `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_INVALID_ACTION_ID`; a chain starting from an anonymous `ref` stays invalid however many named actions it passes through |

This structure is intended for observation only. Abstract hooks should not mutate persistent machine variables through `machine` or any other runtime-owned storage. All generation-time enumerable metadata is numeric on purpose: use the generated macros instead of string comparisons in hot paths.

### `Wrapper::EventContext`

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `event_id` | Queried event id; compare with generated `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_EVENT_*` macros |
| `current_state_id` | Current leaf-state id when the check runs; compare with generated `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_STATE_*` macros |
| `vars` | Read-only snapshot pointer to persistent machine variables |

This structure is the read-only observation surface for event checks. Event- check callbacks should be treated as probes, not as mutation points for persistent machine state. Event and state metadata are numeric so polling code can stay allocation-free and avoid string comparisons.

<a id="build"></a>

## Build

This template is marked experimental. Validate the generated wrapper with your target toolchain and model before deployment.

### Deployment Profiles

`MachineWrapper` owns its C runtime by value; keep it in your application owner, stack or static storage. It is noncopyable. `PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP` removes C heap helper declarations and definitions; it does not change wrapper construction. Define the macro consistently for the C core, wrapper and application. No-heap is not a freestanding guarantee.

### Single-command C++ Poll Wrapper Builds

Compile the C poll core as C99, then compile `machine.cpp` and your C++ application as C++98. The wrapper is designed to work without exceptions, RTTI, STL containers, or default heap allocation.

```bash
gcc -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 -c machine.c -o machine.o
g++ -std=c++98 -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 -c machine.cpp -o machine_cpp.o
g++ -std=c++98 -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 -c app.cpp -o app.o
g++ machine.o machine_cpp.o app.o -lm -o app
```

The equivalent Clang / Clang++ form is:

```bash
clang -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 -c machine.c -o machine.o
clang++ -std=c++98 -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 -c machine.cpp -o machine_cpp.o
clang++ -std=c++98 -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 -c app.cpp -o app.o
clang++ machine.o machine_cpp.o app.o -lm -o app
```

A no-heap C++ poll-wrapper build uses the same profile macro for the C poll core, `machine.cpp`, and every C++ source that includes `machine.hpp`:

```bash
gcc -std=c99 -DPYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 -c machine.c -o machine.o
g++ -std=c++98 -DPYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 -c machine.cpp -o machine_cpp.o
g++ -std=c++98 -DPYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 -c app.cpp -o app.o
g++ machine.o machine_cpp.o app.o -lm -o app
```

On Windows, keep the same source split but use your compiler's equivalent C99, C++98, RTTI, exception, library, and DLL flags; do not add `-lm` for MSVC-style linkers.

### CMake C++ Poll Wrapper Integration

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.5)
project(generated_machine_cpp_poll_app C CXX)

add_library(machine STATIC machine.c machine.cpp)
target_include_directories(machine PUBLIC ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR})
set_target_properties(
    machine
    PROPERTIES
    C_STANDARD 99
    C_STANDARD_REQUIRED YES
    C_EXTENSIONS NO
    CXX_STANDARD 98
    CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED YES
    CXX_EXTENSIONS NO
)

if(MSVC)
    target_compile_options(machine PRIVATE $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/GR->)
else()
    target_compile_options(
        machine
        PRIVATE
        $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:-fno-exceptions>
        $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:-fno-rtti>
    )
    target_link_libraries(machine PUBLIC m)
endif()

add_executable(app app.cpp)
target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE machine)
```

For a no-heap wrapper target, keep the macro public so `machine.h` sees one consistent ABI from `machine.c`, `machine.cpp`, and all consumers:

```cmake
target_compile_definitions(machine PUBLIC PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP)
```

### Integration limits

Serialize access to each instance: it is non-reentrant. Do not call cycle recursively from callbacks. Keep volatile device registers, DMA buffers and I/O inside application adapters and expose numeric readings through input providers. `PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP` removes heap helpers; it is not a strict freestanding guarantee. Standard math, string and diagnostic facilities may still be required.

Before C/C++ deployment, run `pyfcstm inspect` and review numeric diagnostics for the default C/C++ deployment-profile; Python numeric behavior differs. Generated `round()` uses `nearbyint()` and assumes `FE_TONEAREST`. Integer overflow, target ABI and cross-device serialization need application-level review. Compiler and sanitizer evidence does not establish MISRA, AUTOSAR, DO-178C, IEC 61508 or ISO 26262 certification.

### Linkage and Visibility

- `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_API` is the generated symbol-visibility macro used by all public functions.
- Define `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_BUILD_SHARED` when building the generated runtime itself as a Windows DLL.
- Define `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_USE_SHARED` when consuming that DLL from external code on Windows.
- `machine.h` wraps only the public API declarations in `extern "C"` when included from C++.
- Public layout structs, hook typedefs, and event-check typedefs stay usable from both C and C++.
- In generated integer fields, `{{ machine_class_name(model) }}Int` is the public signed 64-bit type alias chosen to stay valid in both C99 and C++98+.

